# A static site

### From a course tree to pages you can open

Tutors Ladder, rung 2 (Lite), talk 1

---

## What rung 2 adds

```
node src/main.ts ../fixtures/ladder-sample-course out
wrote 8 pages and tutors.json to .../out
```

Rung 1's generator, unchanged, plus a renderer:

| File | Job |
| --- | --- |
| `src/html.ts` | `escapeHtml`, the `html` tagged template, `SafeHtml`, the page layout |
| `src/markdown.ts` | a small Markdown renderer |
| `src/site-builder.ts` | `Course` to pages: which pages exist, how each kind looks |
| `src/main.ts` | build, render, then write the files |

Still no dependencies, still nothing to install.

---

## Static, on purpose

A static site is a folder of HTML files. No server, no JavaScript, no build step for the reader:

- open `out/index.html` from disk, or serve the folder with anything
- every link is relative, so the folder can be moved, zipped or published as it is
- the same course, read by people who will never install Tutors

Upstream has the same tool: `tutors website` (`packages/jsr/tutors-lite`) writes `html/` beside the
course, and a lecturer can publish it to any static host.

---

## Pages as data

```ts
// variants/rung-2-lite/src/site-builder.ts
/** One file of the website: a path relative to the output folder, and its HTML. */
export type SitePage = { path: string; html: string };

export function buildSite(course: Course): SitePage[] {
  return pagesFor(course, "", "index.html");
}
```

`buildSite` writes nothing. It returns a list, and only `main.ts` touches the disk:

```ts
// variants/rung-2-lite/src/main.ts
for (const { path, html } of pages) {
  mkdirSync(dirname(join(outDir, path)), { recursive: true });
  writeFileSync(join(outDir, path), html);
}
```

So every test of the site is a test of a function's return value. Rung 3 turns this split into packages.

---

## Which pages exist

```ts
export function pagesFor(lo: Lo, dir: string, up: string): SitePage[] {
  switch (lo.type) {
    case "course":
    case "topic":   return [compositePage(lo, path, up), ...children(lo, path)];
    case "unit":
    case "side":    return children(lo, up);   // shown inside the topic page
    case "talk":    return single(html`${slides(lo.contentMd)}`);
    case "note":    return single(renderMarkdown(lo.contentMd));
    case "lab":     return labPages(lo, dir, up);
    case "web":
    case "archive": return [];                 // a card in the parent page is all they need
    default:        return assertNever(lo);
  }
}
```

The `switch` is over `lo.type`, and `default` narrows `lo` to `never`. Add a kind in `model.ts` and this
function stops compiling until the site knows what pages the kind gets.

---

## The same paths as upstream

| Page | Rung 2 | Upstream `tutors-lite` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| course | `index.html` | `index.html` |
| topic | `topic-01-basics/index.html` | same |
| talk, note | `topic-01-basics/unit-1-intro/talk-1-welcome/index.html` | same |
| first lab step | `.../book-1-first-lab/index.html` | same |
| other lab steps | `.../book-1-first-lab/Setup.html` | same |
| walls of each kind | not built | `talk.html`, `lab.html`, `note.html`, `web.html`, `archive.html` |

Upstream: `emitStaticCourse`, `emitComposite`, `emitLab` in `packages/jsr/gen/src/services/course-emitter.ts`.
A unit has no page there either: `emitUnit` only emits its children, inside the topic's folder.

---

## How each kind looks: the second switch

```ts
// variants/rung-2-lite/src/site-builder.ts
function card(lo: Lo, dir: string): SafeHtml {
  const kind = html`<span class="kind">${lo.type}</span>`;
  switch (lo.type) {
    case "course": case "topic": case "talk": case "note": case "lab":
      return html`<li class="card">${kind}<h3><a href="${dir}index.html">${lo.title}</a></h3><p>${lo.summary}</p></li>`;
    case "web":
      return html`<li class="card">${kind}<h3><a href="${lo.route}">${lo.title}</a></h3><p>${lo.summary}</p></li>`;
    case "archive": // the archive file is not copied in this rung, so it is named, not linked
      return html`<li class="card">${kind}<h3>${lo.title}</h3>...<code>${lo.archiveFile}</code></p></li>`;
    case "unit": case "side":
      return html`<li class="card unit">${kind}<h3>${lo.title}</h3><ul class="cards">${lo.los.map((child) => card(child, `${dir}${child.id}/`))}</ul></li>`;
    default:
      return assertNever(lo);
  }
}
```

Inside `case "archive"`, `lo` is an `Archive`, so `lo.archiveFile` compiles. Inside `case "web"`, it would not.

---

## Exhaustive rendering, upstream and here

```ts
// packages/jsr/gen/src/services/course-emitter.ts (tutors-mono-repo 2b96b69)
async function emitLoPage(lo: Lo, path: string) {
  if (lo.type == "lab") { await emitLab(lo as Lab, path); }
  if (lo.type == "note" || lo.type == "panelnote") { await emitNote(lo as Lo, path); }
  if (lo.type == "tutorial") { await emitTutorial(lo as Lo, path); }
  if (lo.type == "topic") { await emitComposite(lo as Topic, `${path}`); }
  if (lo.type == "talk") { await emitTalk(lo as Talk, `${path}`); }
}
```

A chain of `if`s with casts. A new kind gets no page, and nothing says so. The casts (`lo as Lab`) tell the
compiler to trust the code, where a union lets the compiler check it.

When you add `github` to rung 2, the compiler sends you to `card` and to `pagesFor`. That is L5.

---

## Relative links that travel

Every page links home and up with a path relative to **itself**:

```ts
/** A link from the page at `from` to the page at `to`, both relative to the site root. */
export function relativeHref(from: string, to: string): string {
  return "../".repeat(from.split("/").length - 1) + to;
}

relativeHref("a/b/index.html", "index.html")   // "../../index.html"
```

```html
<!-- topic-02-practice/unit-1-lab/book-1-first-lab/Setup.html -->
<nav><a href="../../../index.html">Home</a><a href="../../../topic-02-practice/index.html">Up</a></nav>
```

No absolute paths and no base URL, so the folder works from `file://`, from a sub-path on a host, or zipped.

---

## A lab is a small site of its own

```ts
function labPages(lab: Lab, dir: string, up: string): SitePage[] {
  const fileOf = (index: number) => (index === 0 ? "index.html" : `${lab.los[index]!.shortTitle}.html`);
  return lab.los.map((step, index) => {
    const steps = lab.los.map((s, i) =>
      html`<li>${i === index ? html`<strong>${s.title}</strong>` : html`<a href="${fileOf(i)}">${s.title}</a>`}</li>`);
    const previous = index > 0 ? html`<a href="${fileOf(index - 1)}">Previous</a> ` : "";
    const next = index < lab.los.length - 1 ? html`<a href="${fileOf(index + 1)}">Next</a>` : "";
    ...
  });
}
```

One page per step, a list of every step with the current one in bold, Previous and Next. Upstream's
`Lab.vto` (`packages/jsr/gen/src/templates/vento/Lab.vto`) does the same with a sidebar.

---

## Talks: slides without Marp

```ts
/** A Marp deck is Markdown with `---` lines between slides; each slide becomes a section. */
function slides(markdown: string): SafeHtml[] {
  return markdown.split(/^---\s*$/m).map((slide) => html`<section class="slide">${renderMarkdown(slide)}</section>`);
}
```

Rung 1 already put the deck's body in the talk's `contentMd`, front matter removed. Rung 2 splits it on
`---` lines. Upstream renders the deck with Marp itself: `renderMarpToStaticHtml` in
`packages/jsr/gen/src/services/marp-renderer.ts`, with Marp's CSS. That needs a dependency, so it waits.

---

## What rung 2 leaves out

- templates (upstream uses Vento), Tailwind, the walls, the table of contents, the breadcrumb trail
- a full Markdown library: tables, math, Mermaid, syntax highlighting, the `::video` directive
- copying images and archives into the site
- Marp rendering
- sanitising: escaping stops injected tags, but a `javascript:` link keeps its URL until rung 6

Each is in `variants/rung-2-lite/RUNG.md`, "Deliberately left out".

---

## Summary

- the site is a list of `{ path, html }`; only `main.ts` writes files
- the paths match upstream `tutors-lite`, so the two sites can be compared page by page
- two exhaustive switches decide how each kind looks and which pages it gets
- relative links make the folder portable

Next: the Markdown renderer.
